1
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:11,780
This module will discuss an approach to interpretation of chest radiographs, beginning with assessment

2
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:17,660
of lines and tubes, the airway, and the mediastinum.

3
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:22,720
Following completion of this module, the learner should be able to employ a systematic search

4
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:29,020
pattern for interpreting chest radiographs, identify the normal position of lines and

5
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:36,420
tubes, and identify alterations in the normal contour and density of the mediastinum.

6
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:42,780
There are multiple approaches to interpretation of chest radiographs, and this is just one search

7
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:48,940
pattern that we are going to discuss here, termed the inside-out approach.

8
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:52,520
The important thing is that you develop your own search pattern that you feel comfortable

9
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:58,840
with, one that is thorough, and that you be consistent from case to case with implementing

10
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:00,600
this search pattern.

11
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:06,600
Now in the inside-out approach, we begin with assessment of lines and tubes, followed by

12
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:12,260
assessment of the trachea and airway, then the mediastinum and heart.

13
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:19,520
We move outward along the diaphragm with assessment then of the pleura, then we compare right and

14
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:28,840
left lungs for symmetry, and then finally end with assessment of the bones and soft tissues.

15
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:36,600
In this module, we are going to discuss the assessment of lines and tubes, the trachea and the airway,

16
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:40,070
and the mediastinum and the heart.

17
00:01:40,070 --> 00:01:45,190
So let's look at how the inside-out approach works on a frontal chest radiograph.

18
00:01:45,190 --> 00:01:49,070
We're going to begin with assessment of lines and tubes, which this patient does not have.

19
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:54,830
Then we're going to start centrally on the inside, looking at the trachea and major airways.

20
00:01:54,830 --> 00:02:01,830
Then we're going to assess the contours of the heart and mediastinum, remembering back to

21
00:02:01,830 --> 00:02:08,830
the normal anatomy modules where we talked about what contributes to all of these interfaces.

22
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:10,830
Then we're going to move to the hyla.

23
00:02:10,830 --> 00:02:20,700
Now that we're central, we're going to move out along the diaphragm, out to the periphery,

24
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:24,700
assessing the lateral costophenic sulci as well as the diaphragm.

25
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:30,700
Then our eyes are going to move up from the lateral costophenic sulci along the edge of the pleura

26
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:34,700
to assess for things like pleural thickening or pneumothoraces.

27
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:42,140
Then we'll start at the lung apex and compare the lung from the apex to the base, right and left,

28
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:43,660
looking for any asymmetries.

29
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:53,020
Then we'll assess the bones, soft tissue, and then underneath the diaphragm, the upper part of the abdomen.

30
00:02:56,560 --> 00:02:59,920
At the end of this search pattern, we're going to do one last check.

31
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:06,880
What we're going to do on this last check is to take a second look at the known blind spots

32
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:08,960
on a frontal chest radiograph.

33
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:13,840
These are the sites at which abnormalities are frequently missed.

34
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:18,960
So we'll do one last check at these blind spots, beginning at the lung apices, comparing

35
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:24,240
right and left. Above the clavicle, lung apices should be well aerated.

36
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:28,720
We're going to look at the hyla and behind the hyla, as well as behind the heart,

37
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:32,160
and then finally behind the diaphragm.

38
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:40,520
On the lateral radiograph, we'll begin our assessment with evaluating the mediastinal contours.

39
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:46,920
Again, thinking back to the normal anatomy and what structures make up the normal mediastinal contours

40
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:53,240
on the lateral view. Then we'll do a check of the clear spaces. So we're going to begin with

41
00:03:53,240 --> 00:04:00,040
the retrotracheal space, or Raider's triangle, the retrosternal clear space,

42
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:03,160
and then finally the retrocardiac clear space.

43
00:04:04,680 --> 00:04:08,520
We'll then move back to assess the posterior costophrenic angles,

44
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:18,120
and then we'll assess the opacity of the posterior aspect of the lungs overlying the spine. So

45
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:20,600
remember that normal gradation as we go from

46
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:27,880
the lung apex to the lung base, we go from radio opaque to more radiolucent.

47
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:34,120
And that's a normal finding that we talked about on a prior module. At the lung apex,

48
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:39,080
you have less lung parenchyma and more soft tissue compared with the base. So you have this

49
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:43,400
normal gradation. So we're looking for maintenance of that gradation.

50
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:50,440
Now let's begin discussion of commonly placed lines and tubes beginning with the endotracheal tube.

51
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:55,720
When you're assessing endotracheal tube position, you really need to identify two things. The

52
00:04:55,720 --> 00:05:03,480
endotracheal tube itself, which you can see as a linear radio opacity, so a line that is

53
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:10,200
marked on the endotracheal tube. And you also need to evaluate or find the position of the corina.

54
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:17,160
So in this example, we can identify the corina here and the tip of the endotracheal tube. If we follow

55
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:24,920
the line down, we see the tip here. Ideally, then the endotracheal tube tip should be placed about

56
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:33,000
two to six centimeters above the corina. In this example, the patient has an endotracheal tube

57
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,960
and complete opacification of the left hemithorax. So why is this present?

58
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:46,280
We can identify the corina. Now as we follow the endotracheal tube caudally, we see the tip of this

59
00:05:46,280 --> 00:05:52,120
is actually below the level of the corina in the right main stem bronchus, accounting for the left

60
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:58,440
lung collapses. The left lung is not getting any of the air because the tip of the endotracheal tube

61
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,480
is caudal to the corina. So only the right lung is being aerated here.

62
00:06:04,920 --> 00:06:08,920
It is important to understand that the endotracheal tube

63
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:15,000
will move with changes in positioning of the head. And this can sometimes be a little bit

64
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,280
counterintuitive, but the easiest way to remember this relationship

65
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:25,560
is that the endotracheal tube tip will follow the chin. So the image on the left is a frontal view.

66
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:31,720
Notice that the patient's chin isn't included in the image, so it's at least neutral or maybe slightly

67
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:39,360
upwards. Here we have the corina in the endotracheal tube tip. Now when the same patient flexes the neck

68
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:45,040
so that the chin is down and can be identified now in the image, notice what happens to the

69
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:51,920
endotracheal tube it actually moves down. Again the endotracheal tube tip will follow the chin. So here

70
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:59,120
we have the corina and notice now how close the endotracheal tube tip is actually to the corina

71
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:06,320
as the patient's neck flexed and the chin move downward. Now let's move on to assessment of central

72
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:12,560
venous catheters. The optimum positioning for these catheters is a little bit controversial and may vary

73
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:17,840
depending on the type of catheter. So the important thing here will be to identify the cavoatrial

74
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:24,800
junction. As in most cases you want the tube tip to be at the cavoatrial junction or just above this

75
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:33,600
in the lower third of the superior vena cava. Some lines however like a tunnel dialysis catheter you

76
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:38,480
may want in the right atrium, but in any case if you can identify the cavoatrial junction you'll have a

77
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:45,760
good estimation of where the tip of the catheter is. The question then becomes how do you identify

78
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:53,200
the cavoatrial junction on a radiograph and this too is controversial but one kind of easy way to estimate

79
00:07:54,160 --> 00:08:03,040
where the cavoatrial junction will be is to first find the corina then count down two vertebral body

80
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:08,160
units below the corina. So you have a vertebral body the intervening disc space and a vertebral body

81
00:08:09,120 --> 00:08:20,330
and that'll give you the approximate location of the cavoatrial junction. Now let's put this into practice to evaluate the

82
00:08:21,850 --> 00:08:25,610
position of this central venous catheter. First we identify the corina

83
00:08:26,250 --> 00:08:30,090
then we'll have to window so that we can see the spine behind the corina

84
00:08:30,090 --> 00:08:36,650
we can identify two vertebral bodies as well as the intervening disc space.

85
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:43,850
Then we take that as the location of the cavoatrial junction and then we find the tip of the catheter here

86
00:08:44,330 --> 00:08:51,530
so we know the tip of the catheter is just below the cavoatrial junction. There are three general

87
00:08:51,530 --> 00:08:58,650
categories of malposition central venous catheters. Catheters can be placed outside of the vascular lumen so they

88
00:08:58,650 --> 00:09:05,930
can be extravascular in location. This is most common in the soft tissues of the neck or chest wall.

89
00:09:05,930 --> 00:09:11,370
You might want to think about an extravascular placement if you're unable to aspirate blood from

90
00:09:11,370 --> 00:09:18,170
the line and the line takes an unusual course or something different than what you typically expect

91
00:09:18,170 --> 00:09:25,130
from standard anatomy. In addition you can have an arterially placed catheter so you meant to place

92
00:09:25,130 --> 00:09:30,970
it in the vein but it was actually placed within an artery and then finally you can have the line placed

93
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:36,570
within a vein that's either a typical vein although not the vein you are targeting so the

94
00:09:38,010 --> 00:09:44,810
line goes into a vein other than the superior vena cava or someone may have underlying variant venous

95
00:09:44,810 --> 00:09:52,970
anatomy and that line may find its way into an anomalous vein. In addition you have to be careful of

96
00:09:52,970 --> 00:10:00,650
coiled catheters because these catheters place the patient at increased risk for vascular perforation

97
00:10:00,650 --> 00:10:10,800
and thrombosis. Here are two examples of malpositioned central venous catheters. The important thing as we go

98
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:18,640
through some of these examples is that you realize that at this stage your job is merely to be aware that

99
00:10:18,640 --> 00:10:26,240
you need to assess the position of lines and tubes and to know whether the position is normal or not.

100
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:33,360
So in the case on the left notice that there is a left pick that does not cross the midline to go into

101
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:43,600
the superior vena cava but instead stays within a vein along the left mediastinum. So this is a left mediastinal

102
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:50,940
catheter placement which has a whole differential diagnosis that we won't go into at this point but

103
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:55,900
again the important point is for you now is just to realize that this is not in the normal position.

104
00:10:56,460 --> 00:11:03,340
In the case on the right we have the left sided central venous catheter that extends cranially

105
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:11,420
within the left internal jugular vein towards the head. In addition to assessing position

106
00:11:11,420 --> 00:11:17,900
of the line you want to look for associated complications. Pneumothorax is the most common

107
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:24,140
complication associated with central venous catheter insertion however there are other complications

108
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:31,260
including pleural effusion or hemothorax as well as vascular perforation or vascular thrombosis.

109
00:11:32,140 --> 00:11:38,220
Now in this example there is a right sided central venous catheter. If we follow the catheter

110
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:45,580
more centrally and we have to window the image appropriately to actually see the catheter tip

111
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:54,700
we can see that it courses immediately adjacent to the aortic arch in a pattern that suggests that this

112
00:11:54,700 --> 00:12:01,900
is placed within an artery and in fact this was arterial placement of the line in the subclavian

113
00:12:01,900 --> 00:12:09,020
artery and eventually into the nominate artery or brachiocephalic artery. The other complication that

114
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:15,340
you want to notice here is that there's a large pneumothorax. So when assessing lines on a chest radiograph

115
00:12:15,340 --> 00:12:21,260
you want to look number one at the position where is the tip and number two what are associated

116
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:28,540
complications specifically whether or not there's a pneumothorax. When assessing the position of a swan

117
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:34,780
GANS catheter the tip should not be peripheral to the interlobar pulmonary artery that forms the hilum.

118
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:41,100
So on the chest radiograph on the left is shown here the tip is right in that interlobar pulmonary

119
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:49,260
artery so kind of right at the limit of how peripheral the catheter should be. Contrast this to the image

120
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:55,900
on the right where the tip is much too peripheral so this catheter needs to be retracted. In assessing

121
00:12:55,900 --> 00:13:02,700
intra-aortic balloon pump positioning you need to identify the tip only the tip of the balloon pump

122
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:10,380
is radio opaque. Ideally the tip should be located at the inferior aspect of the aortic knob which

123
00:13:10,380 --> 00:13:20,860
represents the proximal aspect of the descending aorta as shown in this case. If the tip is positioned too

124
00:13:20,860 --> 00:13:27,660
high you may occlude the carotid arteries and cause a stroke. If it's too low below the celiac or

125
00:13:27,660 --> 00:13:33,420
mesenteric arteries or even at the level of the renal arteries you can cause renal or mesenteric ischemia.

126
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:39,420
So ideally you're looking for that tip right at the caudal margin of the aortic knob in the proximal

127
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:47,820
descending aorta. When assessing chest tube position you want to make sure that the most proximal side port

128
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:55,580
is indicated by the break in the radio opaque line on the tube as shown here. You want to make sure that

129
00:13:55,580 --> 00:14:09,120
that proximal side port is still within the thorax which it is in this case. The endotachial tube tip

130
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:18,520
should be positioned between two and six centimeters above the carina. Following evaluation of the lines

131
00:14:18,520 --> 00:14:25,960
and tubes we're going to begin the inside out approach with assessment of the trachea and central airways.

132
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:32,760
We're going to evaluate the position of the trachea which should be in the midline or slightly deviated

133
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:41,400
to the right. You see that normal kind of mild rightward deviation in patients that don't take a

134
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:46,840
deep breath so you have hypoventilatory changes and the left-sided arch which most of us have will

135
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:52,520
cause a little bit of rightward deviation but in general it should be near the midline. Any major

136
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:58,600
deviation from this suggests mass effect. You also want to evaluate for the patency of these airways

137
00:14:59,160 --> 00:15:05,640
and then finally you need to identify the carina as we talked about for evaluation of the endotracheal

138
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:12,460
tube position. This is an example of tracheal deviation so in our search pattern we're going to

139
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:20,220
find the trachea and assess its position. In this case we see the trachea is deviated to the patient's

140
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:28,620
right and on the CT we can identify the mass in the thyroid gland that is causing this displacement.

141
00:15:30,910 --> 00:15:39,150
In addition to position you want to evaluate the patency of these large central airways. Notice

142
00:15:39,150 --> 00:15:46,350
on the case on the left there's a cutoff or abrupt stoppage of that air column of the right main stem

143
00:15:46,350 --> 00:15:53,150
bronchus in a patient with a tumor occluding the right main stem bronchus. On the image on the

144
00:15:53,710 --> 00:15:59,710
right there's a similar finding with a cut off of the right main stem bronchus in this patient

145
00:15:59,710 --> 00:16:09,070
secondary to a large mucus plug. Following assessment of the position and patency of the trachea and central

146
00:16:09,070 --> 00:16:16,590
airways we'll move on to a more general evaluation of the mediastinum assessing not only the contours

147
00:16:16,590 --> 00:16:24,510
of the mediastinum but also its density. So when assessing the mediastinal contours it's very

148
00:16:24,510 --> 00:16:31,070
important that you have a firm grasp on normal what the normal contours look like and what structures

149
00:16:31,070 --> 00:16:37,390
contribute to these interfaces. The image on the right is a normal chest radiograph showing the normal

150
00:16:37,390 --> 00:16:43,710
concavity of the aorto pulmonary window between the aortic knob and the main pulmonary artery interfaces.

151
00:16:44,750 --> 00:16:52,830
Notice how this concavity is lost and there's a convex opacity in the AP window in this case representing

152
00:16:52,830 --> 00:17:00,800
an enlarged lymph node in a patient with small cell lung cancer. On the chest radiograph on the left

153
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:09,200
notice the abnormal opacity in the low right paratracheal region causing an abnormal interface at this site.

154
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:20,800
This was not present on a chest radiograph performed one year prior and CT confirms that this abnormality

155
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:28,000
reflects an enlarged lymph node. So this is an enlarged lymph node creating an abnormal interface or contour

156
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:35,360
of the mediastinum. In this case we have frontal and lateral radiographs as well as a non-contrast CT.

157
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:42,800
On the frontal radiograph hopefully the abnormality is pretty obvious. We have a large mass causing an

158
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:48,880
abnormal contour of the right side of the mediastinum. On the lateral radiograph remember we have the clear

159
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:56,800
spaces that we check. Notice that the retrosternal clear space is completely opacified so we know that we

160
00:17:56,800 --> 00:18:05,200
have an anterior mediastinal mass that's confirmed on the non-contrast CT in this patient with lymphoma.

161
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:13,520
Here are three examples of aortic pathology resulting in abnormal mediastinal contours.

162
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:22,560
This includes a thoracic aortic aneurysm, an aortic dissection, and then finally an acute traumatic

163
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:29,200
aortic injury. Notice that in addition to causing a contour abnormality the acute traumatic aortic injury

164
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:36,560
because of associated venous bleeding in the mediastinum also causes obliteration or loss of the normal

165
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:42,960
mediastinal contours. Notice how you don't really see a distinct aorta or aortopulmonary window.

166
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:51,440
This is an example of an abnormal mediastinal contour secondary to an enlarged pulmonary artery

167
00:18:51,440 --> 00:19:00,240
as we see on the radiograph on the left. For comparison we have a normal chest radiograph on the

168
00:19:00,240 --> 00:19:07,360
right that shows the normal interface caused by normal size pulmonary artery. So this is an example

169
00:19:07,360 --> 00:19:14,000
of an abnormal mediastinum secondary to an enlarged pulmonary artery in a patient with pulmonary hypertension.

170
00:19:20,240 --> 00:19:26,720
This question tests your ability to identify abnormalities in the contour of the mediastinum

171
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:32,320
and so as part of this you really have to have a firm grasp of the underlying normal anatomy knowing

172
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:39,120
which structures contribute which interfaces to the mediastinum. In terms of the left side of the mediastinum

173
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:45,840
we typically have three or four bumps that we need to identify. So first is the aorta.

174
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:50,880
The second is the main pulmonary artery which in this case is enlarged so that was the answer to this

175
00:19:50,880 --> 00:19:57,440
question there was an enlarged main pulmonary artery. The left atrial appendage which in this case is

176
00:19:57,440 --> 00:20:04,400
really we kind of infer its position because it's not enlarged. And then finally the left ventricle.

177
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:11,680
We also need to distinguish the main pulmonary artery contour which we have labeled here as opposed to the

178
00:20:11,680 --> 00:20:19,960
left pulmonary artery itself which really forms the hylar shadow. As part of our assessment of the

179
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:27,400
mediastinum we need to estimate overall heart size. One way to do this is to calculate the transverse

180
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:38,440
cardiothoracic ratio as defined on a PA radiograph. The transverse cardiothoracic ratio represents the

181
00:20:38,440 --> 00:20:47,320
ratio of the maximum transverse width of the heart labeled A in this image to the maximum transverse

182
00:20:47,320 --> 00:20:57,320
width of the thoracic cavity as measured from inner rib to inner rib. This is labeled B on this

183
00:20:57,320 --> 00:21:04,760
image. So the ratio of the maximum transverse width of the heart to the maximum transverse width of the

184
00:21:04,760 --> 00:21:12,840
thoracic cavity should be less than about 0.5. So in other words the maximum transverse width of the

185
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:20,920
heart should be 50 percent or less of the total transverse width of the thoracic cavity as measured

186
00:21:20,920 --> 00:21:29,080
from inner rib to inner rib. And again remember this is on a PA radiograph which minimizes magnification

187
00:21:30,280 --> 00:21:37,880
of the heart as opposed to an AP radiograph. In addition to assessing overall heart size we can

188
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:44,680
look for findings that are indicative of specific chamber enlargement. So let's start discussion with

189
00:21:44,680 --> 00:21:53,960
findings of left atrial enlargement. Remember the bumps or interfaces along the left mediastinum beginning

190
00:21:53,960 --> 00:22:01,480
with the aorta, the main pulmonary artery, and the left ventricle. Also remember that there's a portion

191
00:22:01,480 --> 00:22:06,920
of the left atrium, the left atrial appendage, this finger-like extension of the left atrium which

192
00:22:06,920 --> 00:22:12,920
contributes to part of the left heart border. Usually you have to just infer its presence because it really

193
00:22:12,920 --> 00:22:20,920
doesn't make a convex outward interface but notice in this case we have a fourth hump or a fourth mogul

194
00:22:20,920 --> 00:22:30,110
and that represents the enlarged left atrial appendage. In addition the left atrium may create an

195
00:22:30,110 --> 00:22:36,590
interface with the adjacent lung behind the heart. Now sometimes this can happen with a normal size left

196
00:22:36,590 --> 00:22:42,750
atrium where you happen to identify this interface but there are measurements that tell you okay is this

